Electrical discharge machining of nickel-based superalloys is sensitive to electrode material because electrode properties affect process efficiency, accuracy, and surface finish. {{cite:S1#c1}} Studies that compare different graphite grades on Hastelloy C-22 report that finer-grained graphite yields lower surface roughness while coarser grains increase material removal rate but also raise relative tool wear. {{cite:S1#c1}} A later investigation that repeats this comparison for negative polarity confirms that fine-grained POCO AF-5 (1 µm) improves process efficiency and reduces tool wear relative to coarser POCO EDM-180 (10 µm). {{cite:S3#c1}}

Experimental work links the dominant EDM parameters to MRR, TWR and Ra: discharge current and pulse duration are repeatedly identified as primary drivers of MRR and Ra, while the off-time (time interval) strongly governs relative electrode wear for graphite electrodes. {{cite:S1#c1}} {{cite:S3#c1}} Taguchi-based factorial analyses in related EDM studies likewise find peak current (amperage) to explain the largest share of variance in MRR and surface roughness in high-performance alloys. {{cite:S2#c1}} {{cite:S8#c1}}

The mechanism by which graphite grain size influences performance is attributed to grain detachment affecting discharge stability: detaching grains perturb the spark behavior and thereby modulate removal efficiency and tool consumption. {{cite:S3#c1}} Surface morphology and electrode end geometry also change under unstable sparking, producing rounded electrode ends from originally sharp square electrodes, which increases wear and dimensional inaccuracy in machined features. {{cite:S4#c1}}

Researchers have tested several process modifications to moderate high TWR and low MRR typical of EDM. Approaches include cryogenic treatment of workpiece or electrode, powder-mixed dielectric, ultrasonic assistance, and combinations such as cryogenic cooling with other aids; these hybrid methods target MRR, TWR, surface integrity and recast layer formation. {{cite:S6#c1}} Evidence from nanopowder- and cryogenically treated electrodes shows substantial reductions in electrode wear and overcut in other material systems, indicating that process environment and electrode treatment are potent levers for improving dimensional accuracy. {{cite:S7#c1}}

Despite the body of work on graphite and on process aids, comparative data on copper and composite electrodes specific to EDM of Hastelloy are not present in the reviewed sources. [[NEEDS SOURCE: information on copper and composite electrodes in EDM of Hastelloy]]

Electrical discharge coating (EDC) literature offers a broader context for modifying surface characteristics via discharge processes, but does not supply direct comparative metrics for conventional versus composite tool electrodes on Hastelloy. {{cite:S5#c1}} Overall, the assembled studies converge on peak current and pulse timing as chief determinants of MRR and Ra, and on electrode material microstructure (graphite grain size) and discharge stability as key factors controlling tool wear. {{cite:S1#c1}} {{cite:S3#c1}}

rows.bRaw:
Electrical discharge machining of nickel‑based alloys is sensitive to the electrode material because the electrode’s physical and mechanical properties influence process efficiency, accuracy and the machined surface state; this influence is emphasised in studies on Hastelloy C‑22 that test graphite electrodes of differing grain sizes. {{cite:S1#c1}} Graphite with coarser grains was found to give higher removal efficiency but also greater relative tool wear, whereas finer‑grained graphite produced lower surface roughness (Ra); empirical models relating machining parameters to MRR, TWR and Ra were derived from these experiments. {{cite:S1#c1}} A later investigation that introduced fine (1 µm) and coarser (10 µm) POCO graphite grades corroborates that fine‑grained graphite improves machining efficiency and reduces tool wear, and reports that detached graphite grains disrupt discharge stability and hence affect process performance. {{cite:S3#c1}}

Across the reviewed work, electrical parameters dominate variations in MRR, TWR and surface finish. Peak current (amperage) and pulse duration are repeatedly reported as primary drivers of MRR and Ra, with studies attributing most of the variance in these responses to current in ANOVA results. {{cite:S1#c1}} Experimental investigations on Hastelloy and other nickel alloys show that pulse duration and current shape both the energy per discharge and the thermal diffusion at the gap, thereby affecting electrode tapering, wear length and edge rounding through unstable sparking and heat diffusion. {{cite:S4#c1}} Taguchi and ANOVA analyses in separate EDM studies likewise identify peak current as the single most influential factor on MRR and TWR, often explaining the majority of variation in those outcomes. {{cite:S2#c1}} Similar findings in machining titanium link amperage strongly to MRR and TWR, reinforcing the central role of current in determining process performance. {{cite:S8#c1}}

Several approaches have been explored to mitigate the principal EDM drawbacks—high tool wear, low MRR and poor surface integrity. Reviews summarise hybrid strategies such as cryogenic treatment, powder‑mixed dielectrics, ultrasonic assistance and combined process modifications as means to improve MRR, reduce TWR and alter recast formation. {{cite:S6#c1}} Experimental work that mixed nanopowders in the dielectric and applied cryogenic treatment to electrodes reports substantial reductions in electrode wear and overcut for brass electrodes under particular dielectric conditions. {{cite:S7#c1}} Separately, electrical discharge coating (EDC) methods extend EDM toward surface modification and coating formation, indicating a broader role for discharge‑based techniques in controlling surface characteristics. {{cite:S5#c1}}

