Selective laser melting (SLM) produces near-full-density components by layerwise melting and fusion of metal powder using a high power-density laser, and therefore generates characteristic thermal histories that strongly influence the resulting microstructure. {{cite:S1#c1}} The rapid cooling intrinsic to SLM gives rise to very fine submicron features: one study reports an average submicron grain size of $0.31\ \mu$m consistent with cooling rates on the order of $10^{7}\ \mathrm{K/s}$ and a martensitic matrix containing dense, needle-shaped nanoprecipitates of Ni$_3$X (X = Ti, Al, Mo). {{cite:S3#c1}} Other reviews attribute the distinct hierarchical (sub)grain structure and fine precipitates produced by laser powder bed fusion to the process acting as an intrinsic, in situ heat treatment that both forms and modifies phases during build. {{cite:S6#c1}}

Heat treatment routes applied after SLM alter precipitate populations, residual stresses and mechanical anisotropy, and thereby change both strength and corrosion-related properties. Systematic characterization of as-fabricated and heat-treated SLM maraging steel shows that post-build aging promotes Ni$_3$X precipitation that accounts for age hardening, while heat treatments relieve residual stress and can reduce layerwise mechanical anisotropy. {{cite:S3#c1}} Comparative studies that apply conventional solution treatment and aging before additional surface treatments (for example, nitriding) find that prior heat treatment influences the morphology and integrity of subsequent layers formed by thermochemical processes: nitriding without prior aging produced cracks in the compound layer, whereas nitriding after prior heat treatment improved wear and corrosion resistance relative to untreated SLM material. {{cite:S2#c1}}{{cite:S2#c1}}

Reversion of martensite to austenite during intercritical tempering has been investigated as a route to recover ductility and to change corrosion performance. Tempering within the mixed $\alpha'+\gamma$ range produces reversed austenite that precipitates preferentially at martensite lath boundaries, driven by local nickel enrichment originating from dissolution of intermetallics. {{cite:S4#c1}} In chloride-containing media, specimens tempered to produce such reversed austenite showed improved pitting behaviour: the intercritically tempered material exhibited a higher pitting potential, reduced corrosion current density and a lower measured corrosion rate in 3.5 wt% NaCl than as-printed, conventionally aged, or homogenized specimens lacking reversed austenite. {{cite:S4#c1}}

Surface condition and residual roughness from SLM also modulate corrosion response. SLM-fabricated maraging steel presents a fine martensitic structure in the as-printed state with a relatively high surface roughness (reported Ra ≈ 10 µm), and electropolishing can substantially reduce roughness (by about 65%) while heat treatment and precipitation state are key drivers of both nano-hardness and corrosion potential. {{cite:S8#c1}}{{cite:S3#c1}} Broad reviews of AM steels note that the unique microstructural evolution during processing can enhance corrosion resistance relative to conventional routes in some cases, but that ductility and fatigue remain challenging and interactions between microstructure, surface condition and corrosion deserve further targeted study. {{cite:S6#c1}}{{cite:S5#c1}}

[[NEEDS SOURCE: Quantitative comparison of pitting rates across studies]]

rows.bRaw:
Selective laser melting (SLM) produces near-full-density maraging steel parts by layerwise melting of powder with a high-power laser, a process that yields very high cooling rates and distinctive microstructures compared with conventional routes. {{cite:S1#c1}} Electron microscopy studies of SLM 18Ni-300 (grade 300) report an ultrafine martensitic matrix with submicron grain size (average ~0.31 μm), consistent with cooling rates on the order of $10^{7}\,\mathrm{K/s}$, and a dense distribution of needle-shaped nanoprecipitates (Ni$_3$X, X = Ti, Al, Mo) inside the martensite which contribute to age hardening. {{cite:S3#c1}} Scanning electron and EBSD characterization has shown that as-printed SLM maraging steel commonly exhibits a fine martensitic structure with limited texture and similar microstructure across build sections (xy, xz, yz). {{cite:S8#c1}}

Heat treatments applied to SLM maraging steel modify both precipitate state and residual stress. Systematic characterization indicates that aging produces the massive Ni$_3$X precipitates which strengthen by Orowan looping and coherency strain, while post-build heat treatments relieve residual stresses and can reduce build-orientation mechanical anisotropy. {{cite:S3#c1}} Studies comparing as-printed, austenitized-and-quenched, and aged conditions find that precipitation controls both hardness and electrochemical behaviour: aging produces peak nano-hardness and influences corrosion potential. {{cite:S8#c1}} Plasma-nitriding experiments performed at temperatures equivalent to aging (520 °C) demonstrate that prior heat treatments affect the morphology and integrity of thermochemical layers: nitriding without prior aging led to cracks in the compound layer, whereas nitriding after suitable prior heat treatment improved wear and corrosion resistance of the AM material relative to untreated AM counterparts. {{cite:S2#c1}}

Austenite reversion (reversed austenite) can be induced by intercritical tempering in SLM maraging steels and has a measurable effect on pitting performance in chloride environments. Intercritical tempering near the $\alpha' + \gamma$ range produced reverted austenite that precipitated primarily at martensite lath boundaries, nucleated by local Ni enrichment after dissolution of intermetallics. {{cite:S4#c1}} Electrochemical testing in 3.5 wt% NaCl showed that specimens tempered at 720 °C with reverted austenite exhibited a higher pitting potential, lower corrosion current density, and lower corrosion rate than as-printed, aged, or homogenized specimens lacking reverted austenite, indicating an improvement in pitting resistance associated with the reversed austenite state. {{cite:S4#c1}}

Reviews of AM steels emphasize that the unique hierarchical (sub)grain structures and fine precipitates formed in AM often enhance strength, wear, and corrosion resistance relative to conventional materials, while challenges remain for ductility and fatigue. {{cite:S6#c1}} Surface condition and processing parameters—such as surface roughness and heat-treatment path—also influence corrosion performance, and electropolishing has been shown to reduce SLM maraging steel surface roughness substantially, with implications for corrosion behaviour. {{cite:S8#c1}}{{cite:S5#c1}} [[NEEDS SOURCE: Quantitative comparison of chloride pitting rates across SLM and wrought maraging steels]]

