Household-level studies converge on four recurring frictions that interrupt the pathway from awareness to rooftop installation: information deficits, procedural complexity, structural constraints, and financial risk perceptions. In Karnataka, qualitative interviews and questionnaires with 70 households identify space and roof-ownership limits as the most frequently cited obstacle among non-adopters, with information gaps and upfront cost following behind. {{cite:S1#c1}} The same study documents a large divergence between anticipated and experienced procedural difficulty: prospective adopters expected complexity at a mean of $5.1$ out of $10$ but reported actual complexity of $2.2$ after installation, indicating that perceived process difficulty is often greater than the lived experience. {{cite:S1#c1}} Bagla also reports that some households describe a "subsidy paradox" in which vendor restrictions, empanelment rules and paperwork reduce the effective value of capital subsidies. {{cite:S1#c1}} A summary statement in that study situates these four frictions as constraining adoption despite Karnataka’s comparatively progressive policy framework. {{cite:S1#c2}}

Studies from other contexts reiterate and nuance these themes. A purposive-sample case study of three villages finds that rural households often view solar PV as low-cost and environmentally friendly but simultaneously doubt its reliability, fear theft, and regard the upfront investment as prohibitively large; the authors recommend targeted awareness campaigns and financing designs that accommodate low-income rural families. {{cite:S2#c1}} Survey work in Patna identifies resident-reported barriers that align with the Karnataka findings and argues that coordinated action across government, energy providers and the public is necessary to raise uptake. {{cite:S3#c1}} Similarly, a large cross-India survey using diffusion-of-innovation constructs reports that perceived complexity, relative advantage, compatibility and observability are significant predictors of household purchase intention for rooftop systems. {{cite:S4#c1}}

Perceptions of subsidies and incentives interact with these frictions. In North Kerala, awareness of government subsidy programs correlates positively with attitudes toward rooftop installation and with intention, alongside environmental concern and cost–benefit perceptions, though the authors note that these variables explain only part of the variance in intention. {{cite:S6#c1}} Empirical work from Karnataka complicates this picture by showing that almost half of adopters (45.8%) would have installed even without the subsidy, while others feel procedural restrictions and vendor rules dilute subsidy value. {{cite:S1#c1}} Local studies of consumer perceptions likewise spotlight trust and technological reliability as determinants of adoption, suggesting that policy measures must address both concrete constraints (roof space, paperwork) and subjective barriers (trust, perceived energy quality). {{cite:S5#c1}} 

Across these studies, evidence is consistent that informational and procedural perceptions frequently overstate actual installation difficulty, but structural limits and upfront finance remain binding for many households; targeted outreach, simplified processes and finance options are recurrently proposed remedies. {{cite:S1#c1}}{{cite:S4#c1}}{{cite:S2#c1}}

rows.bRaw:
Household-level evidence from Karnataka highlights four interlocking frictions between awareness and installation: information gaps, procedural complexity, structural limits on roofs, and perceived financial risk. Bagla reports that even in a progressive policy environment with capital subsidies under PMSGY, adoption remains low; the study used semi‑structured interviews and questionnaires with 70 households in Karnataka (24 adopters, 46 non-adopters) to probe these barriers. {{cite:S1#c1}} Bagla summarises the barriers as informational, procedural, structural and perception-related, noting that anticipated complexity among potential adopters substantially exceeded the complexity actually experienced after installation. {{cite:S1#c2}}{{cite:S1#c1}}

Procedural complexity appears salient in households’ narratives but is experienced differently by adopters and non‑adopters: before installation adopters expected complexity at a mean of $5.1$ out of $10$, while after installation they reported actual complexity of $2.2$, a $57$ per cent reduction. {{cite:S1#c1}} At the same time, some adopters described a "subsidy paradox" in which limitations on eligible panels, vendor empanelment rules and paperwork reduced the practical value of subsidies for many households. {{cite:S1#c1}}

Structural constraints—roof space and roof‑ownership—rank highest among non‑adopters in the Karnataka sample, ahead of information and upfront cost. {{cite:S1#c1}} Complementary evidence from rural contexts shows that households may view PV as attractive on environmental and cost grounds yet worry about reliability, theft and high initial capital outlay, concerns that can deter adoption unless programmes explicitly address them. {{cite:S2#c1}} Surveyed households in Meerut similarly emphasise cost, trust and technological reliability as determinants of adoption decisions. {{cite:S5#c1}}

Perceptions and social‑psychological factors also shape uptake. A TPB‑based study in North Kerala found that environmental concern, awareness of government subsidies and cost–benefit perceptions positively affect attitudes toward rooftop systems, and that attitude, subjective norms and perceived behavioural control significantly predict installation intention, with attitude the strongest predictor. {{cite:S6#c1}} At a broader level, diffusion‑of‑innovation analysis across India finds that perceived relative advantage, compatibility, complexity, observability and other innovation dimensions are associated with households’ purchase intentions, though "trialability" did not predict intent in that sample. {{cite:S4#c1}}

City‑level and rural studies converge on the need for coordinated action: research in Patna argues that overcoming barriers requires collaboration among government, utilities and consumers to build trust and create supportive systems for adoption. {{cite:S3#c1}} Taken together, the literature portrays rooftop uptake as limited less by single factors than by overlapping informational, procedural, structural and perceptual frictions that interact with programme design and local contexts. {{cite:S1#c1}}{{cite:S2#c1}}
